{"input_path":"/home/arc/pvl-dev/fixtures/formula.pdf","page_index":1,"page_count":3,"width":1224,"height":1584,"model_settings":{"use_doc_preprocessor":false,"use_layout_detection":true,"use_chart_recognition":false,"use_seal_recognition":false,"use_ocr_for_image_block":false,"format_block_content":false,"merge_layout_blocks":true,"markdown_ignore_labels":["number","footnote","header","header_image","footer","footer_image","aside_text"],"return_layout_polygon_points":true},"parsing_res_list":[{"block_label":"text","block_content":"subgraph $g_{1}$ into a super-vertex $s_{1}$. There are two parallel edges between $s_{1}$ and $v_{7}$ in Figure 5.","block_bbox":[101,171,590,216],"block_id":0,"block_order":1,"group_id":0,"block_polygon_points":[[101.0,171.0],[590.0,171.0],[590.0,216.0],[101.0,216.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"The graph shrink operator  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{k_1}^{k_2}(\\cdot) $ has several properties which will be useful for computing steiner connectivities. Firstly, applying the operator  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{k_1}^{k_2}(\\cdot) $ preserves the steiner connectivity for all edges in the resulting graph.","block_bbox":[101,218,591,315],"block_id":1,"block_order":2,"group_id":1,"block_polygon_points":[[101.0,218.0],[591.0,218.0],[591.0,315.0],[101.0,315.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Property 1: Given a graph  $ G $ and two integers  $ k_1 \\leq k_2 $, the steiner connectivity of each edge of  $ \\text{GS}_{k_1}^{k_2}(G) $ when computed in  $ \\text{GS}_{k_1}^{k_2}(G) $ is the same as that computed in  $ G $.","block_bbox":[104,322,589,394],"block_id":2,"block_order":3,"group_id":2,"block_polygon_points":[[104.0,322.0],[589.0,322.0],[589.0,394.0],[104.0,394.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Secondly, the steiner connectivity for all edges of  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{k}^{k}(G) $ is k. For example, all edges in Figure 5 have steiner connectivity 3.","block_bbox":[103,400,589,447],"block_id":3,"block_order":4,"group_id":3,"block_polygon_points":[[103.0,400.0],[589.0,400.0],[589.0,447.0],[103.0,447.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Property 2: Given a graph  $ G $ and an integer  $ k $, every edge of  $ \\mathrm{GS}_k^k(G) $ has steiner connectivity  $ k $.","block_bbox":[103,454,589,501],"block_id":4,"block_order":5,"group_id":4,"block_polygon_points":[[103.0,454.0],[589.0,454.0],[589.0,501.0],[103.0,501.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Thirdly, multiple operations of  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{k_1}^{k_2}(\\cdot) $ can be chained together.\n\n\\textbf{Property 3:} Given a graph  $ G $ and four integers  $ k_1 \\leq k_2 $ and  $ k_3 \\leq k_4 $ such that  $ \\max\\{k_1, k_3\\} \\leq \\min\\{k_2, k_4\\} $, we have  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{k_3}^{k_4}\\left(\\mathrm{GS}_{k_1}^{k_2}(G)\\right) = \\mathrm{GS}_{\\max\\{k_1, k_3\\}}^{\\min\\{k_2, k_4\\}}(G) $.","block_bbox":[102,508,592,627],"block_id":5,"block_order":6,"group_id":5,"block_polygon_points":[[102.0,508.0],[592.0,508.0],[592.0,627.0],[102.0,627.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Our Divide-and-Conquer Approach: ECo-DC. From Property 2, we know that the steiner connectivities of all edges of  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{k}^{k}(G) $ are  $ k $. Moreover, from the definitions of steiner connectivity and the graph shrink operator, we know that all edges whose steiner connectivities are  $ k $ will be in  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{k}^{k}(G) $. Thus, to compute steiner connectivities of all edges of  $ G $, it suffices to compute  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{k}^{k}(G) $ for  $ k \\in [1, \\delta(G)] $. Instead of naively computing  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{k}^{k}(G) $ independently for each  $ k \\in [1, \\delta(G)] $ which would take  $ \\mathcal{O}\\left(\\delta(G) \\times \\mathsf{T}_{\\text{KECC}}(G)\\right) $ time, we propose a divide-and-conquer approach based on the fact that  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{k}^{k}(G) $ is entirely contained in  $ \\mathrm{GS}_{k_{1}}^{k_{2}} $ if  $ k_{1} \\leq k \\leq k_{2} $.","block_bbox":[100,641,593,884],"block_id":6,"block_order":7,"group_id":6,"block_polygon_points":[[100.0,641.0],[593.0,641.0],[593.0,884.0],[100.0,884.0]]},{"block_label":"algorithm","block_content":"Algorithm 3: ECo-DC(G)\n\n1 Compute the degeneracy $\\delta(G)$ of $G$;\n2 Compute-DC$(G,1,\\delta(G));$\n3 Construct Hierarchy$(G,sc(\\cdot,\\cdot));$ /* See Algorithm 4 */;\n4 return $\\mathcal{T}$;\n\nProcedure Compute-DC$(g,L,H)$\n\n5 if $L=H$ then\n6 $\\left\\lfloor \\text{for each edge } (u,v) \\in E(g) \\text{ do } sc(u,v) \\leftarrow L; \\right.$\n7 else\n8 $\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l} \\text{Choose an integer } M \\text{ such that } L<M \\leq H; \\\\ \\phi_{M}(g) \\leftarrow \\text{KECC}(g,M); \\quad / * \\text{Compute } M\\text{-ECCs of } g \\star /; \\\\ \\text{Let } g_1 \\text{ be the graph obtained from } g \\text{ by contracting each connected subgraph of }\\phi_{M}(g) \\text{ into a super-vertex, and } g_2 \\text{ be } \\phi_{M}(g); \\quad / * g_1 = \\text{GS}_{L}^{M-1}(G), g_2 = \\text{GS}_{M}^{H}(G) \\star /; \\\\ \\text{Compute-DC}(g_1,L,M-1); \\\\ \\text{Compute-DC}(g_2,M,H); \\end{array}\\right.$\n9 $\\phi_{M}(g) \\leftarrow \\text{KECC}(g,M); \\quad / * \\text{Compute } M\\text{-ECCs of } g \\star /;$\n10 Let $g_1$ be the graph obtained from $g$ by contracting each connected subgraph of $\\phi_{M}(g)$ into a super-vertex, and $g_2$ be $\\phi_{M}(g)$; $/* g_1 = \\text{GS}_{L}^{M-1}(G), g_2 = \\text{GS}_{M}^{H}(G) \\star /;$\n11 Compute-DC$(g_1,L,M-1)$;\n12 Compute-DC$(g_2,M,H)$;","block_bbox":[103,920,586,1290],"block_id":7,"block_order":8,"group_id":7,"block_polygon_points":[[103.0,920.0],[586.0,920.0],[586.0,1290.0],[103.0,1290.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"The pseudocode of our approach is shown in Algorithm 3, denoted ECo-DC. It first computes the degeneracy  $ \\delta(G) $ of  $ G $ (Line 1), and then invokes procedure Compute-DC with input  $ (G, 1, \\delta(G)) $ to compute the steiner connectivities of all edges (Line 2), while Line 3 constructs the hierarchy tree and will be discussed in Section 4.2. The input to Compute-DC consists of a graph  $ g $ and an interval  $ [L, H] $. If  $ L = H $, then the steiner connectivities of all edges of  $ g $ are set as  $ L $ (Lines 5–6). Otherwise, an integer  $ M $ is chosen such that  $ L < M \\leq H $ (Line 8), then the set  $ \\phi_M(g) $ of  $ M $-ECCs of  $ g $ is computed (Line 9) and two graphs  $ g_1 $ and  $ g_2 $ are obtained from  $ g $ based on  $ \\phi_M(g) $ (Line 10), and finally the algorithm continues on  $ g_1 $ (Line 11) and on  $ g_2 $ (Line 12).","block_bbox":[102,1332,592,1423],"block_id":8,"block_order":9,"group_id":8,"block_polygon_points":[[102.0,1332.0],[592.0,1332.0],[592.0,1423.0],[102.0,1423.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"","block_bbox":[630,171,1121,346],"block_id":9,"block_order":10,"group_id":8,"block_polygon_points":[[630.0,171.0],[1121.0,171.0],[1121.0,346.0],[630.0,346.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"We prove by the following lemma that when initially invoking Compute-DC with graph G and interval  $ [1,\\delta(G)] $, the graph g being processed for each recursion with interval  $ [L,H] $ is  $ GS_L^H(G) $.","block_bbox":[630,348,1120,415],"block_id":10,"block_order":11,"group_id":10,"block_polygon_points":[[630.0,348.0],[1120.0,348.0],[1120.0,415.0],[630.0,415.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Lemma 4.1: For Compute-DC, if the input graph $g$ is $\\mathrm{GS}_{L}^{H}(G)$, then the two graphs $g_{1}$ and $g_{2}$ obtained at Line 10 are exactly $\\mathrm{GS}_{L}^{M-1}(G)$ and $\\mathrm{GS}_{M}^{H}(G)$, respectively.","block_bbox":[630,421,1118,499],"block_id":11,"block_order":12,"group_id":11,"block_polygon_points":[[630.0,421.0],[1118.0,421.0],[1118.0,499.0],[630.0,499.0]]},{"block_label":"image","block_content":"","block_bbox":[635,522,1116,729],"block_id":12,"block_order":null,"group_id":12,"block_polygon_points":[[635.0,522.0],[1116.0,522.0],[1116.0,729.0],[635.0,729.0]]},{"block_label":"figure_title","block_content":"Figure 6: Recursion tree","block_bbox":[776,716,977,736],"block_id":13,"block_order":null,"group_id":13,"block_polygon_points":[[776.0,716.0],[776.0,735.0],[976.0,735.0],[976.0,716.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Based on Lemma 4.1, the recursion tree of invoking Compute-DC with input  $ (G, 1, \\delta(G)) $ is as shown in Figure 6.","block_bbox":[630,744,1118,789],"block_id":14,"block_order":13,"group_id":14,"block_polygon_points":[[630.0,744.0],[1118.0,744.0],[1118.0,789.0],[630.0,789.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"The correctness and time complexity of Algorithm 3 are proved by the two theorems below.","block_bbox":[630,790,1120,834],"block_id":15,"block_order":14,"group_id":15,"block_polygon_points":[[630.0,790.0],[1120.0,790.0],[1120.0,834.0],[630.0,834.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Theorem 4.1: Algorithm 3 correctly computes the steiner connectivity for all edges of G.","block_bbox":[630,840,1121,885],"block_id":16,"block_order":15,"group_id":16,"block_polygon_points":[[630.0,840.0],[1121.0,840.0],[1121.0,885.0],[630.0,885.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Theorem 4.2: The time complexity of Algorithm 3 is  $ O(h \\times T_{KECC}(G)) $, where h is the height of the recursion tree in Figure 6.","block_bbox":[630,900,1126,944],"block_id":17,"block_order":16,"group_id":17,"block_polygon_points":[[630.0,900.0],[1126.0,900.0],[1126.0,944.0],[630.0,944.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Near-Optimal Time Complexity. Algorithm 3 correctly computes the steiner connectivities of all edges regardless of the choice of  $ M $ at Line 8, as long as  $ L < M \\leq H $. Yet, the time complexity of Algorithm 3 would vary for different choices of  $ M $. For example, if  $ M $ is always set as  $ L + 1 $ or always set as  $ H $, then the height of the recursion tree would be  $ \\delta(G) $ and thus the time complexity of Algorithm 3 would be  $ O(\\delta(G) \\times \\text{T}_{\\text{KECC}}(G)) $ on the basis of Theorem 4.2. To make the time complexity as low as possible, we will need to reduce the height of the recursion tree. Thus, we propose to set  $ M $ as  $ [\\frac{L+H}{2}] $, and prove in the following theorem that the time complexity of Algorithm 3 then becomes  $ O((\\log \\delta(G)) \\times \\text{T}_{\\text{KECC}}(G)) $.","block_bbox":[629,960,1123,1202],"block_id":18,"block_order":17,"group_id":18,"block_polygon_points":[[629.0,960.0],[1123.0,960.0],[1123.0,1202.0],[629.0,1202.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Theorem 4.3: By setting  $ M = \\left\\lceil \\frac{L+H}{2} \\right\\rceil $, the time complexity of Algorithm 3 is  $ O((\\log \\delta(G)) \\times T_{\\text{KECC}}(G)) $.","block_bbox":[631,1209,1121,1258],"block_id":19,"block_order":18,"group_id":19,"block_polygon_points":[[631.0,1209.0],[1121.0,1209.0],[1121.0,1258.0],[631.0,1258.0]]},{"block_label":"text","block_content":"Following the above theorem, we set  $ M = \\left\\lceil \\frac{L + H}{2} \\right\\rceil $ in Algorithm 3. The time complexity of ECo-DC, which is  $ O\\big( (\\log \\delta(G)) \\times T_{KECC}(G) \\big) $, is optimal up to a logarithmic factor  $ \\log \\delta(G) $. This is because the time complexity of ECo-decomposition cannot be lower than  $ T_{KECC}(G) $, as ECo-decomposition also implicitly computes the k-ECCs of G; specifically, the k-ECCs of G can be obtained from the hierarchy tree in time linear to the sizes of the k-ECCs.","block_bbox":[629,1264,1135,1423],"block_id":20,"block_order":19,"group_id":20,"block_polygon_points":[[629.0,1264.0],[1135.0,1264.0],[1135.0,1423.0],[629.0,1423.0]]},{"block_label":"number","block_content":"1150","block_bbox":[588,1507,637,1528],"block_id":21,"block_order":null,"group_id":21,"block_polygon_points":[[588.0,1507.0],[636.0,1507.0],[636.0,1527.0],[588.0,1527.0]]}],"layout_det_res":{"input_path":null,"page_index":null,"boxes":[{"cls_id":22,"label":"text","score":0.8814187049865723,"coordinate":[101,171,590,216],"order":1,"polygon_points":[[101.0,171.0],[590.0,171.0],[590.0,2